# JWT Token Storage

Never store JWT (or other auth) tokens in `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`. Tokens in web storage are readable by any XSS script. Prefer `httpOnly` cookies with `SameSite=Strict` so the browser sends them automatically and JavaScript cannot access them.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — XSS can steal the token
localStorage.setItem("accessToken", jwt);
sessionStorage.setItem("token", jwt);

// ✅ GOOD — server sets httpOnly + SameSite=Strict cookie
res.cookie("access_token", jwt, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: "strict",
  path: "/",
});
```

## Client usage

- Do not read auth tokens from JS storage or attach them manually from `localStorage`.
- Use `credentials: "include"` (or equivalent) so cookies are sent on API requests.
- Keep token issuance, refresh, and clearing on the server via `Set-Cookie` / cookie expiry.
